{"causes":["Written in the 8th century CE by an unknown author","Influenced Arabic literature and Islamic culture","Inspired translations into many languages"],"effects":["Influenced Western literature","Shaped Islamic cultural heritage","Continues to captivate audiences worldwide with its themes of love, betrayal, and redemption"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Arabic literature","sourceName":"Arabic literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"This literary movement had a profound impact on the development of Arabic literature"},{"sourceSlug":"Islamic culture","sourceName":"Islamic culture","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"The stories within this collection helped shape Islamic cultural heritage"},{"sourceSlug":"Western literature","sourceName":"Western literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"Voltaire's 'Zadig' was influenced by this collection"},{"sourceSlug":"Goethe","sourceName":"Goethe","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"Goethe's 'The Sorrows of Young Werther' was influenced by this collection"},{"sourceSlug":"Scott","sourceName":"Scott","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"Scott's 'Ivanhoe' was influenced by this collection"},{"sourceSlug":"Voltaire","sourceName":"Voltaire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"Voltaire's 'Zadig' quotes from this collection"},{"sourceSlug":"Ottoman Empire","sourceName":"Ottoman Empire","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"This collection was included in the Ottoman Empire's literary canon"},{"sourceSlug":"Persia","sourceName":"Persia","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"One Thousand and One Nights","targetName":"The One Thousand and One Nights","context":"Some translations of this collection were from Persia"}],"places":[{"name":"Mecca","role":"Place of origin"},{"name":"Baghdad","role":"Major adaptation site"},{"name":"Cairo","role":"Translation center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: One Thousand and One Nights","type":"Reference article","slug":"one-thousand-and-one-nights"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q8258","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Thousand_and_One_Nights"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"One Thousand and One Nights is remembered as a landmark text of the Classical era, referenced in 158 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"One Thousand and One Nights (Arabic: أَلْفُ لَيْلَةٍ وَلَيْلَةٌ, Alf Layla wa-Layla) is a medieval Arabic-language frame narrative collection of stories — assembled, compiled, and augmented over several centuries from diverse sources (Indian, Persian, Arabic, and Egyptian folklore) into a composite work that reached its approximate present form in the 14th–15th century CE (Mamluk Egypt), though the earliest Arabic manuscript fragments date from the 9th century CE and the oldest surviving complete manuscript from the 14th century. The collection is framed by the story of the Sasanian king Shahryar — who, having discovered his first wife's infidelity, resolves to marry a new virgin bride each night and execute her the following morning — and Scheherazade (Shahrazad), the vizier's daughter, who volunteers to marry the king and delays her execution by telling him a new story each night, always ending at a suspenseful moment to ensure the king keeps her alive to hear the conclusion. The frame continues for one thousand and one nights until the king, transformed by the stories, spares Scheherazade's life.

The collection contains the most famous stories of the medieval literary world — Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves, Sinbad the Sailor (seven voyages), Aladdin and the Magic Lamp, the tale of the Three Apples (often cited as the earliest detective story), and hundreds of others — stories that range from court intrigue, merchant adventures, and voyages of discovery to love stories, ghost stories, comic tales, and philosophical parables. The collection demonstrates the extraordinary diversity of the Arabic narrative tradition — drawing on Indian tale collections (the Panchatantra tradition), Sasanian Persian stories (the Hazar Afsana, 'Thousand Fictions'), and original Arabic material in a synthesis that exemplifies the cosmopolitan cultural exchange of the medieval Islamic world.

The One Thousand and One Nights was introduced to Europe by Antoine Galland's French translation (Les Mille et Une Nuits, 1704–1717) — a free and embellished translation that immediately became a literary sensation and inaugurated the European fashion for Orientalism. Several of the most famous stories (Aladdin, Ali Baba) are not found in any known Arabic manuscript and were probably added by Galland himself. The collection has subsequently shaped European literature (Byron, Goethe, Dickens, Poe, Proust, Borges, Rushdie), Hollywood cinema, and world popular culture.","subjects":["Arabic Literature","Medieval Era","World Literature","Frame Narrative","Folklore","Islamic Culture","Scheherazade","Cultural Transmission"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":10,"significanceNarrative":"One Thousand and One Nights (medieval Arabic, compiled 8th–14th centuries CE) is one of the most influential narrative collections in world history. Its characters (Sinbad, Aladdin, Ali Baba, Scheherazade) are among the most globally recognisable fictional figures, its Scheherazade frame structure influenced the theory of narrative, and Galland's French translation (1704–1717) inaugurated European Orientalism. A testament to the cosmopolitan literary culture of medieval Islam and a foundational work of world popular culture.","significanceCategory":"world-changing"},"_editLog":[]}
